From: Yocto build fix
Subject: [PATCH] aasdk: use proto2 for touch messages so zero-valued fields aren't dropped

Every one of aasdk_proto's .proto files declares `syntax="proto3"`. Proto3's
wire format has a well-known property: a singular scalar or enum field left
at its default (zero) value is not serialized at all -- there is no way on
the wire to distinguish "field explicitly set to 0" from "field never set".

This is fatal for TouchLocation/TouchEvent specifically, because their
zero-valued cases are not edge cases here, they are the *common* case:
- TouchLocation.pointer_id is always literally 0 in this single-touch
  head unit (see InputService::onTouchEvent -- touchLocation->
  set_pointer_id(0)), so it is omitted from literally every touch message
  sent.
- TouchAction.PRESS == 0 (see TouchActionEnum.proto), so touch_action is
  omitted from the wire on every single press -- the message that starts
  every tap.

This exact failure mode -- touchscreens that work everywhere except inside
an active Android Auto session, despite the host-side code being otherwise
correct -- is a well-documented, previously-diagnosed issue in the wider
openauto/aasdk/crankshaft community (see opencardev/crankshaft#352 and
f1xpl/aasdk#32): newer/stricter Android Auto (Play Services) versions parse
incoming messages strictly enough that a touch event missing these fields
is silently dropped, with no error surfaced anywhere on the host side --
which matches exactly what on-device testing here showed after two earlier,
separate bugs (a cross-thread event-filter race and a double-scaled
coordinate transform, both fixed in the openauto recipe) were fixed: touch
coordinates land in valid range, PRESS/DRAG/RELEASE framing is clean, sends
report no errors, yet the phone shows zero visible reaction.

The community's fix was "convert proto3 to proto2 and recompile", since
proto2 requires (and honors) an explicit `optional` qualifier on every
scalar/enum field, giving real wire presence regardless of value -- matching
what Google's actual Android Auto protocol (specified before proto3 existed)
expects. This is more targeted than a wholesale conversion of the whole
aasdk_proto library: only TouchLocationData.proto and TouchEventData.proto
actually have scalar/enum fields whose legitimate value is their own zero
default, so only those two need converting. Protobuf fully supports proto2
messages importing proto3-syntax enums (TouchActionEnum.proto is left
untouched) and being referenced from proto3-syntax parent messages
(InputEventIndicationMessage.proto's `data.TouchEvent touch_event` field),
so this does not require touching anything else.

The vendored protoc here is 3.12.4, older than the 3.15 release that added
proto3's own `optional` keyword (which would have avoided a syntax switch
entirely), so that shortcut isn't available -- proto2 is the only option.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [oe-specific: works around Android Auto/Play
Services becoming stricter about proto3's implicit-zero-value field
omission than this ~2018 protocol implementation assumed; a well-documented
issue in the wider openauto/aasdk community]

diff --git a/aasdk_proto/TouchLocationData.proto b/aasdk_proto/TouchLocationData.proto
--- a/aasdk_proto/TouchLocationData.proto
+++ b/aasdk_proto/TouchLocationData.proto
@@ -16,13 +16,13 @@
 *  along with aasdk. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 */
 
-syntax="proto3";
+syntax="proto2";
 
 package f1x.aasdk.proto.data;
 
 message TouchLocation
 {
-	uint32 x = 1;
-	uint32 y = 2;
-	uint32 pointer_id = 3;
+	optional uint32 x = 1;
+	optional uint32 y = 2;
+	optional uint32 pointer_id = 3;
 }
diff --git a/aasdk_proto/TouchEventData.proto b/aasdk_proto/TouchEventData.proto
--- a/aasdk_proto/TouchEventData.proto
+++ b/aasdk_proto/TouchEventData.proto
@@ -16,7 +16,7 @@
 *  along with aasdk. If not, see <http://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.
 */
 
-syntax="proto3";
+syntax="proto2";
 
 import "TouchLocationData.proto";
 import "TouchActionEnum.proto";
@@ -26,6 +26,6 @@
 message TouchEvent
 {
     repeated data.TouchLocation touch_location = 1;
-    uint32 action_index = 2;
-    enums.TouchAction.Enum touch_action = 3;
+    optional uint32 action_index = 2;
+    optional enums.TouchAction.Enum touch_action = 3;
 }
